        "body": "A house colour is never applied alone. Any element painted from a house sets both `--house` and `--on-house` (via an `.h-<house>` class) and uses them together: field and text, or inverted (text-coloured chip on a field) on hover. White text goes on Red, Blue, Green and Gray; dark ink on Yellow and Orange.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Field Rule",
        "body": "House colours are applied at full strength as solid fills. No tints, gradients, transparency or glow on a house field. The only translucent fills are the photo-caption shade and the pipeline steps inside a house tile.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two-Ground Rule",
        "body": "Each theme defines its own house values; dark mode lifts the houses slightly rather than dimming them, so the fields stay as loud on the night ground as on paper. Any new house-coloured text must clear 4.5:1 in both themes, not only in light.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Width Means Weight Rule",
        "body": "Expanded width (112–125%) is reserved for black-weight numerals and headings. Labels and small text drop back to 100% width.",
